WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.589
The Mandalorian might be barely halfway through
its inaugural season — but that hasn't stopped

00:00:05.589 --> 00:00:09.540
Baby Yoda from becoming the subject of intense
internet speculation.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:14.020
We've run across a couple of theories that
have us contemplatively stroking our chins

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.740
and saying, "Hmmm... fascinating, this is.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.820
Something to this, there could be."

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.280
These are the most intriguing of them all.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.740
If there's one thing we can say with reasonable
certainty about Yoda's species — which George

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:32.220
Lucas famously insisted not be named — it's
that they're pretty rare.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:37.820
Other than Yoda, and now the so-called Baby
Yoda, we've only ever seen one: Yaddle, a

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.050
female of the species who was shown to be
a member of the Jedi High Council in Star

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:43.820
Wars Episode I: The Phantom Menace.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.960
The fact that all three of these critters
are Force users is provocative in and of itself,

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.400
which we'll discuss more in a moment.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:55.760
But many fans have quite reasonably wondered
if Baby Yoda could actually be Yoda's son,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.320
an idea which we find to be unlikely.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:02.880
The thing about the Yoda's Secret Love Child
Theory — which is a combination of words

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:07.560
we never thought we'd say — is that it would
take a serious leap of logic to make it fit

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.470
with the events of the original trilogy.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:14.660
Sure, Baby Yoda is 50 years old, and therefore
would have been around when Yoda was alive;

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:18.860
The Mandalorian, remember, takes place five
years after the events of those films.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.159
It's conceivable that Yoda could have sent
his wee offspring away to protect him, as

00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:27.920
was done with the infants Luke and Leia, before
the original trilogy's events — but it's

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.640
a bit of a stretch to buy that the Jedi Master
would never mention the tot to Luke, not even

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:33.640
on his deathbed.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:38.140
No, we find it more likely that there's a
different, though just as direct, connection

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.160
to Yoda: that Baby Yoda is a clone of the
late Jedi Master.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.960
This theory is lent credence by the fact that
in The Mandalorian's third episode, the Client's

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.470
employee Doctor Pershing was seen to be wearing
a patch with an insignia suspiciously similar

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:57.840
to that worn by the many, many clones of Jango
Fett seen in Episode II: Attack of the Clones.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:03.180
It sure seems like that fact has to be setting
up something — but if Baby Yoda himself

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.160
isn't a clone, perhaps it's setting up something
else.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.500
"This is what was causing all the fuss?"

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:13.200
Other than the fact that there aren't a whole
lot of them, there's something else we feel

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.860
comfortable inferring about Yoda's species:
they appear to be natural Force users.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:22.830
In The Mandalorian's second episode, Baby
Yoda levitated a ferocious Mudhorn the size

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:27.140
of a bus; sure, it took a lot out of him,
but the point is that we're pretty sure he

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.720
wasn't taught this ability.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.280
The question, then, is: just how rare is this
species?

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:37.720
Hey, here's another one: if they were so rare
as to be, say, on the verge of extinction,

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.901
doesn't it seem likely that several parties
on both sides of the Light Side/Dark Side

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:44.560
divide would have an interest in preserving
them?

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.380
One fan theory states that Baby Yoda is, in
fact, the last of his kind — and that he's

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:54.100
not a clone, but rather, the Client's interest
in the little guy is to clone him.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.560
This theory is bolstered by a line the Client
spits at the hapless Doctor Pershing in The

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:03.569
Mandalorian's third episode, as Mando eavesdrops
on the conversation in advance of his daring

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:08.420
rescue of Baby Yoda: " … extract the necessary
material and be done with it."

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:13.650
Pershing then replies that someone, it's not
crystal-clear who, quote, "explicitly ordered

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:18.710
us to bring it back alive," which, of course,
raises a whole new set of questions — the

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:21.240
most obvious being, who is he referring to?

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.180
Unless that conversation is a total red herring,
we find the clone theory to be pretty credible

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.220
— but there might also be something else
going on here.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.560
Stay with us here, because this theory is
a bit silly — even if it sounds pretty Star

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:35.420
Wars-y on paper.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.260
"Good chair, good chair.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:38.379
nice lumbar support.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.920
Great view of… utter blackness."

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:46.420
If Baby Yoda isn't a clone, then what kind
of "material" would the Client be extracting,

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:47.420
and for what purpose?

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:52.400
Well, we know that Yoda had an insanely high
midi-chlorian count — so its stands to reason

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:56.920
that even if Baby Yoda is only loosely related
to the late Jedi Master, the little guy's

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:59.000
count is probably in the same range.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.330
His "genetic material" then — perhaps even
his blood — might be of use to a Force-sensitive

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:09.400
being whose connection to the Force has been
lost or diminished, or who is very near death,

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:09.900
or both.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:13.060
Okay, so you probably know who we're talking
about.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:17.340
Is it possible that the unknown party who
ordered the Client to bring Baby Yoda back

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:20.120
alive is none other than Emperor Palpatine?

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:25.520
The very same Big Bad who is going to be returning
to vex our heroes in Rise of Skywalker?

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.960
Could the little tyke, in fact, be the key
to his big comeback?

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.660
It sure is a compelling theory, but frankly,
we'll be shocked if it's true.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:38.180
Not because it doesn't make narrative sense;
it'd be awesome for The Mandalorian to tie

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:41.260
in to Rise of Skywalker in such a crazy fashion.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:45.160
We just don't think there's any way that the
two productions could have synced up well

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:49.520
enough to make any crossover possible, let
alone one that would be so important to the

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:50.520
movie's narrative.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.860
Having said that, it's not outside the realm
of possibility.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:59.180
We know, thanks to director Colin Trevorrow
that J.J. Abrams had the idea to bring Palpatine

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:04.380
back into the Skywalker Saga sometime after
September 2017, which is when Trevorrow departed

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:05.120
the project.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:10.580
Disney CEO Bob Iger announced that The Mandalorian
was in development in November of that year,

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.960
and Jon Favreau was introduced as the series'
writer and showrunner in March 2018.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:19.660
Favreau didn't waste any time getting to work;
by May of that year, he told multiple outlets

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:23.420
that half of the scripts for the first season
of The Mandalorian had been completed.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.940
This means that sometime between the fall
of 2017 and the spring of 2018, Favreau and

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:33.000
Abrams would have to have gotten together
and hammered out all of the shared story elements

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.320
of their respective projects.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:38.240
Then, they would have needed to remain in
contact, making sure that neither of them

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.380
was stepping on the others' toes — all while
keeping the whole thing a total secret.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:46.970
Again, this wouldn't have been impossible,
but it sure would have been difficult — and

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:51.169
nobody would know this better than Favreau,
who directed the first two Iron Man movies

00:05:51.169 --> 00:05:55.650
and has appeared as Happy Hogan in the films
of the intricately connected Marvel Cinematic

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:56.960
Universe ever since.

00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:01.260
We're going to go ahead and say that Baby
Yoda is not likely to factor into the story

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.040
of Rise of Skywalker at all.

